1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,222
JAMIE: I'm watching the US government ban Anthropic's best AI models while the people who built them are having a full meltdown—and A M D's up because apparently chip stocks profit from chaos.

2
00:00:11,222 --> 00:00:32,647
ALEX: The Nasdaq is up two point nine six percent, S&P up one point eight seven. A M D's the big story—up seven point fifteen percent—but that's just the chip sector riding broader moves. Meanwhile, oil's down, Exxon down three point four seven, and the entire pharma complex is selling because geopolitical risk just repriced and nobody wants exposure to supply chain chaos.

3
00:00:32,647 --> 00:00:40,809
JAMIE: So we're in this weird spot where the same news that makes energy stocks toxic makes tech stocks move in the other direction.

4
00:00:40,809 --> 00:01:05,294
MAYA: Right—and it's not even that the deal's done, it's just that traders are now betting it might happen, which means they're selling oil futures like they're clearing out a storage unit before the rent goes up. That's the kind of move you get when the market decides 'maybe Iran doesn't stay sanctioned forever,' and every energy CEO suddenly has to explain to their board why their reserve estimates just became fiction.

5
00:01:05,294 --> 00:01:21,958
ALEX: Meta's up five point sixteen, Oracle up five—so the real money's pouring into anything that can scale without needing a drilling permit or a clinical trial. The ten-year Treasury dropped, which is the Fed saying 'yeah, maybe we're done hiking,' and that's the actual engine under this rally.

6
00:01:21,958 --> 00:01:42,023
MAYA: So the thing nobody's talking about is that Anthropic's models are the exact ones cybersecurity teams would use to stress-test their own defenses—like, you're trying to find the hole in your software, and the government just locked away the tools that would let you do it. Security researchers signed a letter saying this ban makes their job impossible.

7
00:01:42,023 --> 00:02:00,047
ALEX: The export control logic is that these models are dual-use—could theoretically be weaponized. But the people actually defending infrastructure against weaponization are saying the restriction does the opposite of what it claims. Which is the classic move: regulate something you don't understand by banning the thing that would help people understand it.

8
00:02:00,047 --> 00:02:09,569
JAMIE: So Anthropic gets hit with this ban, their models are off-limits to US-based security teams, and meanwhile competitors move faster with fewer restrictions. Who actually wins here?

9
00:02:09,569 --> 00:02:25,213
MAYA: Nobody. American companies get slower at finding their own vulnerabilities, foreign competitors move faster with fewer restrictions, and regulators get to tell Congress they 'did something' about AI without having to explain why that something makes everyone less secure. It's regulation as a press release.

10
00:02:25,213 --> 00:02:44,597
MAYA: A two-year-old ended up in the hospital after a car crashed into a nursery playground. A woman was arrested on suspicion of dangerous driving. And look, I've covered a lot of weird stories, but there's something uniquely horrifying about the one place where you're supposed to be safe from vehicles being, well, not safe from vehicles.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cy4eldkpxgwo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

11
00:02:44,597 --> 00:03:04,322
JAMIE: So the playground is supposed to be the one zone where the only thing moving at speed is a toddler on a tricycle, and instead you've got a car doing the same job. How does that even happen—is this a gate failure, a brake failure, or just someone who shouldn't have been anywhere near the parking area?

12
00:03:04,322 --> 00:03:14,524
JAMIE: On the tape tomorrow—we've got earnings rolling in. Powerfleet, Canopy Growth, Comtech Telecom, and Nobility Homes all reporting on June fifteenth. Alex, anything macro shaking loose before that hits?

13
00:03:14,524 --> 00:03:24,046
ALEX: Nothing scheduled between now and then, which means we're just watching the tape react to broader repricing and waiting to see if energy stocks find a floor.

14
00:03:24,046 --> 00:03:30,508
MAYA: So Anthropic's security models are now contraband for the people who'd actually use them to defend against attacks.

15
00:03:30,508 --> 00:03:34,589
ALEX: It's export control via self-sabotage—and the foreign teams are taking notes.

16
00:03:34,589 --> 00:03:39,350
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
